@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' SPEC source: internal/provider/roles.go (RolesFromCanonical) · cmd/proveo/main.go (mergeRoles)
' Record of a plan-vs-implementation drift found by the batch-3 prose migration, and the
' decision taken: the spec was amended to describe what ships.
title DECIDED — A remembered model id is kept verbatim, not dropped (spec amended to match source)

node "What ships today" as SHIPPED <<host>> {
  component "cmd/proveo mergeRoles\nfills only roles the operator did not set" as MERGE <<app>>
  component "provider.RolesFromCanonical\ncopies every non-empty id through:\nno catalog lookup · no warning · no drop" as COPY <<app>>
}

node "What two diagrams claimed" as CLAIMED <<host>> {
  component "internal/provider/model-resolution\n""a cached id that no longer resolves\ndegrades to a warning and is dropped""" as D1 <<app>>
  component "_plans/harness-choice-cache\nthe same sentence, same claim" as D2 <<app>>
}

database "~/.proveo/agent-settings\.yml\ntargets.<t>.models — canonical ids" as CACHE <<db>>
component "Two opposite rules inside _spec\nif the prose had been migrated as written" as SPLIT <<error>>
component "Rejected for now: implement the drop\n(catalog lookup at merge time + a warning path)" as DROP <<error>>

CACHE ARROW_MAIN MERGE : remembered roles
MERGE ARROW_MAIN COPY
COPY ARROW_ERROR D1 : contradicted
COPY ARROW_ERROR D2 : contradicted
D1 ARROW_ERROR SPLIT
D2 ARROW_ERROR SPLIT
CLAIMED ARROW_OPTIONAL DROP : the other way to resolve it

note bottom of COPY
  **The code, verified line by line.** ""RolesFromCanonical""
  (""internal/provider/roles\.go:104"") walks ""RoleVars"" and assigns every
  remembered value that is non-empty after trimming. There is no catalog
  lookup in that path, so "no longer resolves" is a state it cannot
  observe, and nothing warns or drops.

  ""mergeRoles"" then applies a remembered role **only** where the operator
  set nothing, so an exported ""ARCHITECT_MODEL"" is never contradicted and
  one explicit role does not discard the two remembered beside it. That
  half was always accurate in both diagrams.
end note

note right of SPLIT
  **How it surfaced, and why nothing was written.** The batch-3 prose
  migration (""_spec/_plans/prose-to-spec-migration\.puml"") assigned the
  ""mergeRoles"" comment to these two diagrams. Migrating it as written would
  have placed keep-verbatim next to drop-with-warning in ""_spec"" — two
  opposite rules for one code path — so the block was left whole in the
  source and escalated instead of half-trimmed. It is the only block in
  batch 3 that was refused.

  Worth naming the general trap: **a diagram titled PLANNED is not
  evidence of behaviour.** Both of these were, and both had been read as
  description for long enough that the source comment looked like the
  stale side. The drift guard compares fingerprints, not semantics, so
  only reading the two together catches this class.
end note

note bottom of MERGE
  **The decision: the spec now describes keep-verbatim.** The reasoning in
  the source comment stands on its own — proveo cannot tell a catalog that
  is //behind// from a model that is //gone//, and the harness that owns the
  model list produces the better error than a launcher guessing from a
  table it may not have synced.

  What survives from the old sentence is its real constraint: proveo must
  not hard-fail on a file it wrote itself. Keeping the id honours that as
  well as dropping it would have.
end note

note bottom of DROP
  **Not implemented, and recorded so the option is not lost.** Dropping a
  stale id would need a catalog lookup at merge time, a warning path for
  the dropped role, and both diagrams reverted to their earlier wording.

  The argument for it: a remembered id that no longer exists sends the run
  to the harness only to fail there. The argument against is the one that
  won: proveo's catalog is synced from the harnesses (""sync-models""), so it
  is the side more likely to be stale, and dropping on its say-so would
  discard a model that actually works.
end note
@enduml
